<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Salabikh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Salabikh is an archaeological site in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, about 20 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, whose ancient name is uncertain. Excavations found a large archive of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets from about 2600–2500 BCE, including some of the earliest known copies of Sumerian literature, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/instructions-of-shuruppak">Instructions of Shuruppak</a> and the Kesh Temple Hymn, as well as lexical lists.</p></div>
